.TH HSH 1 "January 2026" "1.0" "Simple Shell Manual"
.SH NAME
hsh \- simple UNIX command line interpreter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hsh
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hsh
is a simple UNIX command line interpreter that executes commands read from
standard input or from a file.
.PP
The shell displays a prompt and waits for the user to type a command. A
command line always ends with a new line. The prompt is displayed again
each time a command has been executed.
.PP
The shell works in both interactive and non-interactive mode.
.SH INVOCATION
.B Interactive mode:
When
.B hsh
is started with its standard input connected to a terminal (as determined
by isatty(3)), it displays a prompt and waits for user input.
.PP
.B Non-interactive mode:
When
.B hsh
is started with its standard input not connected to a terminal, it reads
and executes commands from standard input.
.SH COMMAND EXECUTION
After a command is parsed, the shell checks if the command is a built-in.
If it is not a built-in, the shell searches for a command with that name
in the directories listed in the PATH environment variable.
.PP
If the command contains a slash (/), the shell executes the command at
the specified path without searching PATH.
.SH BUILT-IN COMMANDS
The following built-in commands are supported:
.TP
.B exit
Exit the shell. Usage:
.B exit
.TP
.B env
Print the current environment variables, one per line.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B hsh
returns the exit status of the last command executed, or 127 if the
command is not found.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PATH
A colon-separated list of directories in which the shell looks for commands.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Interactive mode:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ./hsh
$ ls -la
$ /bin/pwd
$ env
$ exit
.fi
.RE
.PP
Non-interactive mode:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ echo "ls -la" | ./hsh
$ cat commands.txt | ./hsh
.fi
.RE
